    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "By Vieta's formulas, we know that $a + b + c = 1,$\n$a+b+c = \\frac {ab}{ac} + \\frac {bc}{ac} + \\frac {ac}{ab} = \\frac {3}{ac},$\n$a+bc + c = \\frac {ab}{ac} + b + \\frac {ac}{ab} = \\frac {11}{ab}.$\nThus, $b = \\frac {ac}{ab}$ and $c = \\frac {11}{ab} - a - b$.\nSubstituting, we get $ac = \\frac {ac}{ab} + \\frac {11}{ab} - 2a - b$.\nThis simplifies to $ab(ac + 11 - 3ab) = b^2$.\nDividing both sides by $ab,$ we get $11 - 3ab = b/a$, or $ab + 3b = 11.$\nAdding $a + c = 2$ to both sides, we get $ab + ac + bc = 11 + 2 = \\boxed{13}.$. The answer is 13",
