      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "Let $f (x) = 3x^3 - 3x^2 + 11x - 8.$  Then the roots $a,$ $b,$ $c$ of the given polynomial satisfy\n\\begin{align*}\nf (a) &= 3 (a)^3 - 3 (a)^2 + 11 (a) - 8\\\\\n     &= (a)^3 - (a)^2 + 11 (a) - 8\\\\\n     &= f (c),\n\\end{align*}and similarly $f (b) = f (c).$  Now $f (x)$ is a cubic polynomial, so it can have at most one more root $d$ with $f (d) = f (c).$  Since $f (a) = f (c),$ by the Factor Theorem, $a - c$ and $a - d$ both divide $f (x)$; and thus \\[f (x) = (x - c)(x - d) g (x),\\]where $g (x)$ is a quadratic, since $f (x)$ has a double root $a$.  Since $\\boxed{d = b}$, $g (x) = x^2$.\n\nTherefore,\n\\[f (x) = (x - c)^2 (x^2) = 3 x^3 - 3 x^2 + 11 x - 8.\\]By Vieta's formulas, \\[ab + ac + bc = a b + b c + c a = (a + b + c) b = 8 \\ne 0,\\]since $ab + ac + bc = 8.$\n\nTherefore, the value of $ab + ac + bc$ is $\\boxed{8}.$. The answer is 8",
